<%#
  The one thing this design asks of a care receiver: yes or no, once, on their
  own device.

  Consent did not disappear when pairing did — it moved from before setup to
  first use. A monitoring tool set up on somebody without their knowledge is a
  recognised pattern in elder abuse, and the old flow prevented it by accident,
  because access could not exist unless the person receiving reminders acted.
  This screen is that same guarantee, made on purpose: nothing has been spoken,
  nothing recorded, and the caregiver has seen nothing about this person's day
  until the button below is pressed.

  Naming the caregiver is the point of the first sentence. It is the one thing a
  stranger could not know, and it is what tells somebody whether this is their
  daughter or a scam.
%>
<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
  <div class="text-center">
    <p class="text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">
      <%= @arranger_name %> set this up for you
    </p>

    <p class="text-3xl text-gray-700 mb-4">
      Remindly can remind you about things during the day, out loud, on this device.
    </p>

    <p class="text-2xl text-gray-600 mb-12">
      Nothing has started yet. It is up to you.
    </p>

    <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-4">
      <%= button_to "Yes, start my reminders", start_voice_reminders_path,
            class: "btn-primary text-2xl px-8 py-6" %>

      <%= button_to "No thank you", decline_voice_reminders_path,
            class: "btn-secondary text-2xl px-8 py-6" %>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-xl text-gray-500">
      If you say no, everything <%= @arranger_name %> set up is deleted.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
